+# Hegel property-based testing report for `crop`
+
+Environment: rustc 1.95.0, `hegeltest` 0.28.2 (added with `cargo add --dev
+hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`; crop pins `rust-version = "1.85"`,
+hegeltest needs 1.86).
+
+Shared generators live in `tests/common/mod.rs`:
+
+- `text_with_line_breaks()` — arbitrary Unicode text with boosted density of
+  LF / CRLF / lone CR, the Unicode "line break" code points crop must *not*
+  treat as breaks (NEL, VT, FF, LS, PS), and supplementary-plane /
+  multi-codepoint-grapheme characters (`𐐀`, `🐸`, `🇷🇸`, `🐻‍❄️`, `ß`).
+- `large_text()` — up to ~96KB of text (a drawn seed repeated to a drawn
+  target length), to force multi-level B-trees: integration tests link the
+  crate with 2048-byte leaves and arity 16, so small inputs never leave the
+  root.
+- `rope_text()` — `one_of!` of the two above.
+- `draw_char_boundary` / `draw_char_range` — hegel-drawn byte offsets
+  snapped up to char boundaries (same snapping the crate's own fuzz target
+  uses).
+- `str_byte_of_line()` — reference implementation of `byte_of_line()`
+  computed directly on a `&str`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Property | Test | Evidence |
+|---|----------|------|----------|
+| 1 | **Stateful model test**: a `Rope` under arbitrary insert/delete/replace sequences (full Unicode, at char boundaries) stays equal to a `String` model; after every step `assert_invariants()` holds and `byte_len`/`line_len` match the model; interleaved query rules check `byte()`, `is_char_boundary()`, `line_of_byte()`, `byte_of_line()`, `line()`, and `byte_slice()` against reference implementations computed from the `String` | `rope_edits_match_string_model` (`tests/rope_replace.rs`, `#[hegel::state_machine]` on `RopeVsString`) | The crate's own fuzz target `fuzz/fuzz_targets/editing.rs` runs exactly this Rope-vs-String comparison; per-method docs give the panic contracts and semantics for each query rule |
+| 2 | Repeatedly byte-slicing a `Rope`/`RopeSlice` matches the equivalent `str` slice, and slices satisfy `assert_invariants()` | `byte_slices_match_str_slices` (`tests/slicing.rs`) | Existing seeded-rand test `byte_slice_random` (same file); `byte_slice()` docs |
+| 3 | `line_slice(start..end)` equals the text between the starts of lines `start` and `end`, terminators included | `line_slices_match_str_slices` (`tests/slicing.rs`) | `line_slice()` docs and examples; existing `line_slices_random` |
+| 4 | Concatenating `chunks()` reproduces the original text | `iter_chunks_concat_matches_original` (`tests/iterators.rs`) | Crate-level docs write a `Rope` to disk chunk-by-chunk |
+| 5 | `lines()` agrees with `str::lines()` in both directions | `iter_lines_match_str_lines` (`tests/iterators.rs`) | Fuzz target asserts `rope.lines()` == `string.lines()`; `Lines` implements `DoubleEndedIterator` |
+| 6 | Concatenating `raw_lines()` (terminators included) reproduces the original text | `iter_raw_lines_concat_matches_original` (`tests/iterators.rs`) | `raw_lines()` docs; `line_slices_random` relies on this to compute line offsets |
+| 7 | `chars()` agrees with `str::chars()` in both directions | `iter_chars_match_str_chars` (`tests/iterators.rs`) | Existing `iter_chars_*` tests; `Chars` implements `DoubleEndedIterator` |
+| 8 | A `RopeBuilder` fed arbitrary fragments (CR/LF fragment edges boosted so CRLF pairs straddle `append()` calls) equals the concatenated `String`, equals `Rope::from` of it, and has the right `line_len()` | `builder_matches_appended_text` (`tests/rope_builder.rs`) | Existing `builder_0`, `builder_crlf_0/1`, `builder_line_len` |
+| 9 | `String` -> `Rope` -> `to_string()` is the identity over full Unicode | `rope_from_str_to_string_roundtrip` (`tests/rope_builder.rs`) | `From<&str>` + `Display` impls; fuzz target's rope-vs-string equality |
+| 10 | `line_of_byte(byte_of_line(l)) == l` for every existing line `l < line_len()` | `byte_of_line_then_line_of_byte_roundtrip` (`tests/rope_indexing.rs`) | `byte_of_line()` docs ("byte offset of the start of the given line") |
+| 11 | Per its documented contract, `line_of_byte(offset)` accepts any `offset <= byte_len()` and returns the number of LFs before it | `line_of_byte_documented_panics` (`tests/rope_indexing.rs`) — **FAILING, kept deliberately** (see Suspected bugs) | `line_of_byte()` "# Panics" section lists only out-of-bounds; its example queries an offset in the middle of a `"\r\n"` pair |
+| 12 | `utf16_len()` equals the sum of `char::len_utf16()` over the text (Rope and RopeSlice) | `utf16_len_matches_char_sum` (`tests/utf16_conversion.rs`, `utf16-metric` feature) | Feature docs: "the UTF-16 code units they'd have if their content was stored as UTF-16" |
+| 13 | `utf16_code_unit_of_byte(b)` equals the UTF-16 length of the text before char boundary `b` | `utf16_code_unit_of_byte_matches_model` (same file) | Method docs + existing `byte_to_utf16_*` examples |
+| 14 | `byte_of_utf16_code_unit` inverts `utf16_code_unit_of_byte` at every char boundary | `byte_of_utf16_code_unit_matches_model` (same file) | Method docs + existing `utf16_to_byte_*` examples |
+| 15 | Concatenating `graphemes()` reproduces the original text | `graphemes_concat_matches_original` (`tests/graphemes.rs`, `graphemes` feature) | `graphemes()` docs |
+
+All tests were added to the existing test files for the code they exercise;
+no new test files were created. The existing seeded-`rand` tests were left
+in place; the hegel tests cover the same ground with engine-controlled,
+shrinkable randomness and broader (full-Unicode, mixed-line-break) inputs.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test` (default features): **137 passed, 1 failed** — the only
+  failure is `line_of_byte_documented_panics`, kept failing deliberately
+  (suspected bug #1 below). This includes the stateful model test and all
+  default-feature hegel tests.
+- `cargo test --features graphemes,utf16-metric --test graphemes --test
+  utf16_conversion`: **24 passed, 0 failed** (includes hegel tests #12-15).
+- Liveness check per the skill: temporarily inverted one plain property
+  (chunks concat) and one machine invariant (byte_len); both failed and
+  shrank to minimal counterexamples (`s = ""`); reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 15 hegel tests, then
+  reverted): no additional failures on either feature set.
+
+Note: builds were run with `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0`
+purely because the machine's disk was nearly full; this does not affect
+results.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `line_of_byte()` panics on non-char-boundary offsets, contradicting its documented panic contract
+
+- **Minimal counterexample (as shrunk by hegel):**
+
+  ```
+  let s = "\u{80}";
+  let offset = 1;
+  thread panicked at src/rope/metrics.rs:352:20:
+  byte offset 1 is not a char boundary: it is inside '\u{80}' (bytes 0..2) of "\u{80}"
+  ```
+
+  i.e. `Rope::from("\u{80}").line_of_byte(1)` panics.
+
+- **Why I believe it is a library bug, not a test bug:** the "# Panics"
+  sections of both `Rope::line_of_byte` (src/rope/rope.rs:526-531) and
+  `RopeSlice::line_of_byte` (src/rope/rope_slice.rs:455-460) list exactly
+  one condition — offset greater than `byte_len()`. crop's docs are
+  otherwise meticulous about boundary panics: `insert()`, `byte_slice()`,
+  `byte_of_utf16_code_unit()` etc. all explicitly document "doesn't lie on
+  a code point boundary" panics. The `line_of_byte` example even
+  demonstrates querying a mid-line-break offset ("between the '\r' and the
+  '\n'"), reinforcing that arbitrary interior offsets are meant to be
+  queryable. So the behavior and the documented contract genuinely
+  disagree.
+
+- **Exact code path:** `Rope::line_of_byte` ->
+  `Tree::convert_len::<RawLineMetric>` ->
+  `RawLineMetric::measure_up_to` (src/rope/metrics.rs:346-355), which
+  unconditionally calls `GapBuffer::assert_char_boundary`
+  (src/rope/gap_buffer.rs:297-317). The check is a real `panic!`, not a
+  `debug_assert`, so it fires in release builds too.
+
+- **Likely resolution direction (for the maintainer, not applied here):**
+  the deliberate, nicely-formatted panic message — and the tested
+  intentional panic of the sibling `utf16_code_unit_of_byte` on mid-char
+  offsets (`byte_to_utf16_5` in tests/utf16_conversion.rs) — suggest the
+  *behavior* is intended and the *docs* are incomplete for both
+  `Rope::line_of_byte` and `RopeSlice::line_of_byte`. Either the docs
+  should gain the code-point-boundary panic clause, or the assertion should
+  be dropped for this read-only query (ropey, for comparison, accepts any
+  byte index here). Per instructions I did not change either; the failing
+  test `line_of_byte_documented_panics` pins the documented contract, and
+  the model test's `check_line_of_byte` rule is constrained to char
+  boundaries (with a comment referencing the failing test) so the state
+  machine can keep hunting for deeper bugs.
+
+No other suspicious behavior was found: the full edit-sequence model test
+(including ~96KB multi-level trees, CRLF fixups across chunk boundaries,
+and all index conversions) agrees with the `String` model at 10x the
+default case count.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. One property was **narrowed** after
+investigation: the model machine's `check_line_of_byte` rule originally
+drew *any* offset `<= byte_len()` (justified by the docs) and immediately
+hit the panic above. Since the panic appears to be intended behavior with
+incomplete docs, the rule now draws only char-boundary offsets, and the
+original full-domain property lives on as the deliberately-failing
+`line_of_byte_documented_panics` test rather than being silently weakened.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and the Rust reference were accurate; the whole suite
+(15 hegel tests including a 9-rule state machine, composites in a shared
+module, `one_of!`, `sampled_from`, size-drawn `vecs`) compiled on the
+first attempt with zero API friction. Specific notes:
+
+1. **The MSRV gotcha in the reference paid off exactly as written.**
+   `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed against crop's `rust-version =
+   "1.85"`; the reference's "retry with `--ignore-rust-version`" was the
+   fix. Zero time lost.
+
+2. **Missing: where shared generators go for Rust *integration* tests.**
+   Each file in `tests/` is a separate crate, so per-file test modules
+   can't share composites. The idiomatic Rust answer (a `tests/common/`
+   module, which crop already had) worked perfectly — `#[hegel::composite]`
+   functions defined there are usable from every test binary — but the
+   reference never mentions the pattern. A sentence in the Rust reference
+   ("put reusable composites in the existing shared test module, e.g.
+   `tests/common/mod.rs`") would remove a moment of doubt.
+
+3. **Missing: passing `TestCase` to helper functions.** The reference's
+   method table shows `draw(&self, ...)`, which implies plain helpers like
+   `fn draw_char_boundary(tc: &hegel::TestCase, s: &str) -> usize` work —
+   and they do, including inside `#[rule]` methods that receive `tc` by
+   value. But no example shows a helper taking `&TestCase`, and it's a
+   pattern model tests need constantly (drawing values that depend on
+   current machine state). Worth one example. Relatedly, draws made inside
+   such helpers show up in counterexamples as `let draw_1 = 1;` rather
+   than a named binding — harmless, but unexplained.
+
+4. **Missing: a pattern for very large text inputs.** The skill correctly
+   pushes "deep structure needs large inputs" and the reference covers
+   drawing collection sizes separately, but there's no guidance for
+   multi-kilobyte *strings* (needed here because release-config leaves
+   hold 2048 bytes: no small input ever leaves the root node). I used
+   draw-a-seed-and-repeat-to-a-drawn-target-length, which shrinks well and
+   is cheap; `text().min_size(50_000)` felt like it might be slow and I
+   didn't risk it. A recommended idiom (and a note on the cost of huge
+   `text()` draws) would help.
+
+5. **Missing: what `text()` actually generates.** The reference doesn't
+   say whether `text()` covers full Unicode or how often structurally
+   interesting characters (newlines, `\r`, surrogate-pair-needing chars)
+   appear. For a line-oriented library the property tests are only as good
+   as the line-break density, so I built a boosted composite defensively.
+   One sentence on `text()`'s distribution would let users judge when
+   boosting is necessary.
+
+6. **Gotcha 13 (no global case-count override) is accurate and the
+   workaround is tedious.** The 10x exploratory run required sed-editing
+   15 attributes and reverting. An env-var override in hegeltest itself
+   would make the skill's own step-6 instruction much cheaper to follow.
+
+7. **State-machine ergonomics were excellent.** The failure output
+   (initial draws, then `Step N: <rule>` with per-rule draws) made the
+   `line_of_byte` diagnosis instant, and shrinking reduced it to a 1-step,
+   2-byte counterexample on the first run. The reference's stateful
+   section was sufficient to write a 9-rule machine without trial and
+   error.
+
+8. **Minor ambiguity in SKILL.md step 4:** for existing tests that use
+   *seeded* `rand` (crop has many), it says "the randomness should come
+   from hegel instead", which reads as "replace the existing tests". I
+   added hegel equivalents alongside instead of deleting maintainer tests,
+   since deleting upstream coverage seemed wrong for a contribution.
+   Clarifying intent (evolve in place vs. add a PBT next to it and leave
+   the original) would help.
